1 _DSCR SKETCHES OF THE LIFE OF GEORGE VAUGHN THOMPSON AND LUCIA SPALDING THOMPSON
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Life of George Vaughn Thompson and Lu
2 CONC cia Spalding Thompson&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
2 CONC </line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Contributed By CuffWilliamDee1 &amp;middot; 2013-07-09 16:07:58 GM
2 CONC T+0000 (UTC) &amp;middot;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><lin
2 CONC e>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;SKETCHES OF THE LIFE OF GEORGE VAUGHN THOMPSON AND LUCIA SPALDING THOMPSON b
2 CONC y Lucretia King (daughter of Henry King and Mary Ellen Thompson King and great-granddaughter of George Vaughn Thompson) (Edited for grammar and spelling by William Dee Cuff)&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&q
2 CONC uot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-sma
2 CONC ll;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;George Vaughn Thompson, father of DeWitt Clinton Thompson, was born May 30, 1798 at Elizabethtown, Essex County, New York.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;fo
2 CONC nt-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&qu
2 CONC ot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;He married Lucia Spalding, 1 January 1822. She was the daughter of Jabez Spalding and Mary Spalding, born 2 March 1796, Windsor, Berkshire County, Massachuse
2 CONC tts. They were the parents often children, all born in Alexandria, Genesee County, New York. The children are: Ward Spalding, Cyrenus Wilford, George Delos, Levi Ozro, Obed V., Laura Althea, DeWitt Clinton, Ellen Diadema,&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;
2 CONC p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;
2 CONC &quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Mary Janet, and Lucia I.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;f
2 CONC ont-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;They left their home for Utah in Bishop Hunter&apos;s Company, October 1849 and wintered at Council Bluff. They continued their journey 17 July 1850 and arrived in Salt Lake City, Utah, 12 October 1850. Thei
2 CONC r first winter in Utah was spent at a place called Little Pigeon. While crossing the plains they endured many hardships. The cattle were stampeded by Buffalo breaking many of the wagons and destroying their supplies. At one time the cattle were los
2 CONC t and DeWitt Clinton went in search of them. He lost his way in the woods, and all night he walked back and forth beating off the wolves with clubs. His Mother waited all night long for him to return, never ceasing to pray for his safety. When morn
2 CONC ing came a company of men went in search of him. They found him completely exhausted. The club he had been using was in shreds. A number of the cattle died and they had to yoke up the cows. Ellen Diadema, sister of DeWitt Clinton had to drive as he
2 CONC r father had mountain fever and was ill a long time.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Their hardships were numerous, but the saddest thi
2 CONC ng they had to endure was leaving most of their children as only three came with them to Utah.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;DeWitt C
2 CONC linton&apos;s Mother read her bible daily; she believed that wherever the book opened was the passage she should read. When DeWitt Clinton was 19 years old, he made three trips back to the Missouri river to get immigrants.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt
2 CONC ;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small
2 CONC ;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;They made their home in Big Cottonwood. After George Vaughn passed on Lucia lived with DeWitt Clinton until he moved his famil
2 CONC y to Marysvale, Piute County, Utah. Then she lived with Ellen Diadema the remaining years of her life.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;
2 CONC The world has known many distinguished people, but where do we find a parallel to the 3,000 Latter Day Saints who traveled as far as 1500 miles, across oceans, prairies, deserts, and mountains, not in quest of gold or other earthly riches, but in s
2 CONC earch of a home where they might live in peace and worship God according to the dictates of their own conscience. A parallel cannot be found in the history of the world&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&
2 CONC gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: xx-small;&q
2 CONC uot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;(Information obtained from Delilah Gardner - Edah Maxfield Allred and Mary Ellen Thompson King)&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
